Z||000020||V8Dreamer||06-21-2001||10:34 AM||||Thanks BV, those were the #'s I was hopin to hear......now it's gonna be a 3800 II as long as I can find one.....just gotta decide on auto or manual.<P>Ryan||||reg||14
Z||000021||Toddster||06-21-2001||11:58 AM||||V8Dreamer, It helps to see the crank but I don't have a picture. I'll try to let words work. The crank on the 60 degree v-6 has a very strong design because it has one journal separating each set of rods. The 3.8 has a 'staggared' design which (in the middle) separates two opposing rods with no journal between them. The amount of metal betwee these two rods is about a 1/4 inch. At 7000 RPM you have approxiamately 12,000 foot pounds of force acting in opposition to each other. I've seen a number of these cranks sheared right off at that weak point. Buick Grand Nationals and Turbo Buicks litter the wrecking yards with giant holes in the oil pans from launching pistons into the ground.<P>The 3.8 (3800) is a time tested engine and very streetable, but if it's power that you want, I'd go for the V-8.<p>[This message has been edited by Toddster (edited 06-21-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000022||V8Dreamer||06-21-2001||04:03 PM||||Toddster, <P>Thanks for the info...I'll consider that in my choice.<P>Ryan||||reg||1
Z||000023||BV MotorSports||06-21-2001||06:31 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Toddster:<BR><B>V8Dreamer, It helps to see the crank but I don't have a picture. I'll try to let words work. The crank on the 60 degree v-6 has a very strong design because it has one journal separating each set of rods. The 3.8 has a 'staggared' design which (in the middle) separates two opposing rods with no journal between them. The amount of metal betwee these two rods is about a 1/4 inch. At 7000 RPM you have approxiamately 12,000 foot pounds of force acting in opposition to each other. I've seen a number of these cranks sheared right off at that weak point. Buick Grand Nationals and Turbo Buicks litter the wrecking yards with giant holes in the oil pans from launching pistons into the ground.<P>The 3.8 (3800) is a time tested engine and very streetable, but if it's power that you want, I'd go for the V-8.<P>[This message has been edited by Toddster (edited 06-21-2001).]</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Ryan,<P>Go to the local j-yard(s) and look for FWD GM cars (mid-size-up) and look at how many miles they have. Remember most are cookie cutter mom's cars that have had horrible maint. on them. My buddies 89 3.8 Bonneville has had the oil changed like 10 times in its life and has 184k on it. No B.S. Smokes like hell but its still running even if 1/2 the car shouldnt be alowed on the road!<P>Get with Ken Deltwhiller (spl?) or even Art Carr They are famous for making 3.8's FLY!<BR>They could tell you more than probably any of us on this msg board. They make a living around the 3.8 so they would know.<P>Good luck.<P>Also, check out Chris West's page...good info on the different swaps.<P>Steven<P><P>------------------<BR>87 Fiero GT 14.9/ 93mph<BR>94 Formula WS6 w/ NOS 12.4 /118mph<BR>87 Conquest TSi <BR>85.5 Starion ESi||||reg||1
Z||000024||88formula||06-21-2001||06:51 PM||||Some say the 3800 engine is going to be the next SBC of the future. I have driven a fiero with a SC3800 engine and the five speed Getrag transmission and let me tell you I was very impressed with the torque that thing had. Even at 40 mph in overdrive (5th) it could really pull.<P>Skip the NA 3800 and spend the extra bucks on the SC3800. You won’t be disappointed.  If I could do it all over again I would do this swap. They are reliable too. With just a simple under drive pulley on the blower they make 280 horsepower. If you do the traditional engine modifications (headers, heads, cams) I don’t see why you can’t make over 300. I don’t know much about the Cadillac 4.5 except that I have driven one and they move pretty well. That was a long time ago though.    <BR><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
